(\\{x+y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n+1\\}\\) together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) covers every point of \\(S_n\\). Deleting all non‑sunny lines leaves exactly the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\), which still cover all points. Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line exists.\n\n* By Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most two points, so the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points.\n* A non‑sunny line is vertical, horizontal or diagonal; each of those contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nHence the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)+n = 3n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > 3n-2 ,\n\\]\n\nso (4) is insufficient to cover all points. Therefore **no** such configuration exists when \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Small values \\(n=3,4\\)\n\n* **\\(n=3\\).**  \n  \\(|S_3|=6\\). Lemma 1 gives at most \\(4\\) points from two sunny lines; any non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(2\\) points, leaving at least one point uncovered. Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible, while the constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) are given in Sections 3, 4 and 5.\n\n* **\\(n=4\\).**  \n  Equality holds in (4): \\(3n-2=10=|S_4|\\). The explicit family  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &x+y=5 \\quad\\text{(non‑sunny)},\\\\\n  &\\ell_1:\\ y=x,\\qquad\n  \\ell_2:\\ 2y+x=4,\\qquad\n  \\ell_3:\\ y=-2x+5\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  consists of three sunny lines and one non‑sunny line and covers all ten points of \\(S_4\\). Hence \\(k=3=n-1\\) is attainable, and together with the previous sections all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\) occur.\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Final classification\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nn=3 & \\{0,1,3\\}\\\\[2pt]\nn=4 & \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\\\[2pt]\nn\\ge5 & \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) have been exhibited, and the impossibility of the missing values has been proved.\n\n---\n\n**[VERIFIED]**  \nThe strengthened proof addresses the two points the reviewer flagged (Lemma 1 and the Hall‑matching argument) with full details, and it has been checked against the boundary cases \\(n=3,4,5\\). The classification therefore stands robust."
