nny lines that could cover the remaining two points are vertical, horizontal or diagonal, each of which would miss at least one of the uncovered points.\n\n   *\\(n=4,\\;k=3\\) attainable*:  \n   - Use the diagonal line \\(x+y=5\\) (non‑sunny) to cover the four points \\((1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)\\).  \n   - Cover the remaining six points by the three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\ell_1 &: y=x \\quad\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n     \\ell_2 &: 2y+ x =4 \\quad\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\n     \\ell_3 &: y = -2x+5 \\quad\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1).\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n     None of these three lines is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   The resulting family consists of exactly three sunny lines and one non‑sunny line, i.e. \\(k=3=n-1\\).\n\nCombining the existence results for \\(k\\le n-2\\) and \\(k=n\\) with the impossibility for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge5\\) (and the explicit small‑\\(n\\) analysis) yields the complete classification stated in the boxed table.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of required points  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) the line \\(x+y=t\\) contains exactly \\(t-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\). Summing over \\(t\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nA line is *sunny* iff its direction vector is **not** a scalar multiple of  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1).\n\\]\n\nConsequently the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nconsist entirely of non‑sunny lines.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on exactly three lines of (2): \\(x=a\\), \\(y=b\\) and \\(