(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\), at most two of them can belong to the same line. ∎\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Existence of configurations with \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nConsider the bipartite graph  \n\n\\[\nG=(V,H,E),\\qquad \nV=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\},\\; H=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\},\n\\]\n\nand place an edge \\((x=i,y=j)\\) in \\(E\\) iff \\((i,j)\\in S_n\\) (i.e. \\(i+j\\le n+1\\)).\n\nFor any subset \\(A\\subseteq V\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n|N(A)|\\ge |A|-1,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nbecause the points with \\(x\\)-coordinates in \\(A\\) occupy at most the rows \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) and the condition \\(i+j\\le n+1\\) can eliminate at most one column from the neighbourhood.  \nInequality (3) is precisely Hall’s condition for the existence of a matching that leaves at most one vertex of \\(V\\) unmatched. Hence \\(G\\) possesses a matching of size \\(n-2\\).\n\nLet \\(M=\\{(a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_m,b_m)\\}\\) be any matching with \\(m\\le n-2\\).  \nStarting from the non‑sunny covering \\(\\mathcal C\\) of Section 3, for each edge \\((a_i,b_i)\\) we **replace** the vertical line \\(x=a_i\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal C\\)) by a sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) that passes through \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and a second point of \\(S_n\\) not already used on a sunny line.  \n\nBecause the two points are distinct, the slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); thus \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n\nThe point \\((a_i,b_i)\\) remains covered (by \\(\\ell_i\\)).  \nAny other point that was covered **only** by the removed vertical line must have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(a_i\\); such a point \\((a_i,b)\\) satisfies \\(a_i+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore also lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a_i+b\\), which is still present in the family. Hence coverage is preserved.\n\nAfter performing the replacement for all \\(m\\) edges we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) lines containing precisely \\(m\\) sunny lines. Since a matching of any size \\(m\\le n-2\\) exists, we have constructed configurations for **every** \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\)\n\nTake the perfect matching  \n\n\\[\nM^{*}=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\subset S_n .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\((i,n+1-i)\\) choose a second point of \\(S_n\\) distinct from all previously chosen second points (this is always possible because \\(|S_n|>2n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\)). Draw the line \\(\\ell_i\\) through the pair; its slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(\\ell_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nThe diagonal lines \\(x+y=t\\;(2\\le t\\le n+1)\\) together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) cover every point of \\(S_n\\). Deleting all non‑sunny lines leaves exactly the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\), which therefore still cover all of \\(S_n\\). Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\n#### 7.1  Impossibility for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nLet a configuration contain exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(S_n\\); therefore the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points.  \n\nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or diagonal.  \nFor a vertical line \\(x=i\\) the admissible points satisfy \\(i+b\\le n+1\\), giving at most \\(n+1-i\\le n\\) points; the same bound holds for a horizontal line, and a diagonal line \\(x+y=t\\) contains exactly \\(t-1\\le n\\) points.  \n\nConsequently the single non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points.  \nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)+n=3n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>3n-2,\n\\]\n\nso (4) is insufficient to cover all points. Thus **no** configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists when \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n#### 7.2  Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n* \\(n=3\\).  \n  Here \\(|S_3|=6\\). Two sunny lines can cover at most \\(4\\) points, and any non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(2\\) points (the longest vertical or horizontal line inside the triangle has length \\(2\\)). Hence at most \\(6\\) points could be covered, but a direct inspection shows that the two uncovered points can never lie on the same non‑sunny line; consequently \\(k=2\\) is impossible. (All six points are listed in the analysis; any choice of two sunny lines leaves a pair that is not collinear with a vertical, horizontal or diagonal line.)\n\n* \\(n=4\\).  \n  Equality holds in (4): \\(3n-2=10=|S_4|\\). The explicit construction given in the **Method Sketch** (three sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) together with the diagonal line \\(x+y=5\\)) shows that \\(k=3=n-1\\) **is** attainable.\n\nThus the only values of \\(k\\) that fail to occur are:\n\n* \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\);\n* \\(k=2\\) for \\(n=3\\).\n\nAll other non‑negative integers \\(k\\le n\\) are realizable.\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Final classification\n\nCollecting the results:\n\n* **\\(n=3\\)**: admissible \\(k\\) are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n* **\\(n=4\\)**: admissible \\(k\\) are \\(\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\) (all values).\n* **\\(n\\ge5\\)**: admissible \\(k\\) are \\(\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\\).\n\nThis completes the determination of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
