y.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nn & \\text{admissible }k\\\\ \\hline\nn=3 & \\{0,1,3\\}\\\\\nn=4 & \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\\\\nn\\ge 5 & \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) are attainable except the single value \\(k=n-1\\); the exceptional value does occur for \\(n=4\\) (where it is attainable) and for \\(n=3\\) (where it is not attainable).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Counting the required points.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) lattice points.\n\n2. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   The three families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consist respectively of vertical, horizontal and diagonal lines; none of them is sunny.\n\n3. **A covering by only non‑sunny lines.**  \n   By a greedy deletion argument we can select exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines from \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) that still cover all points of \\(S_n\\). Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **A bound for sunny lines (Lemma 1).**  \n   Any sunny line contains **at most two** points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Proof sketch*: three collinear points of \\(S_n\\) would force the line’s direction to be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, contradicting sunniness.\n\n5. **Existence for \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Using Hall’s marriage theorem we obtain a matching of size \\(n-2\\) in the bipartite graph whose left vertices are the vertical lines \\(\\{x=i\\}\\) and right vertices the horizontal lines \\(\\{y=j\\}\\), with an edge \\((x=i,y=j)\\) whenever \\((i,j)\