actly \\(t-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\); summing over \\(t\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A covering by non‑sunny lines  \n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal N_n=\n\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n* The lines \\(x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1)\\) are vertical,\n* \\(y=1\\) is horizontal,\n* \\(x+y=n+1\\) is diagonal.\n\nNone of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are **non‑sunny**.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies one of the following:\n\n* if \\(a\\le n-1\\) then it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\);\n* if \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n* in all cases \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so the point also lies on the diagonal\n  line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nHence the family \\(\\mathcal N_n\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Sunny lines can contain many points  \n\nA line is *sunny* iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of\n\\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).  \nFor the slope‑\\(1\\) line \\(y=x\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\subset S_n .\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line may contain up to \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(S_n\\); in particular the statement “a sunny line contains at most\ntwo points of \\(S_n\\)” is false.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Replacing a non‑sunny line by a sunny one  \n\nWe describe a replacement that preserves the covering property.\n\n*Vertical line.*  \nTake a vertical line \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\).  \nIts points in \\(S_n\\) are  \n\n\\[\nP_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose the two extreme points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\) of \\(P_i\\).\nThe line through them has equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-(n-i)\\,x+(n+1-i).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(-\\dfrac{n-i}{1}\\neq