e following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **The point set.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **A covering with no sunny line.**  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_0=\n   \\bigl\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{y=1\\}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which are **non‑sunny** (they are\n   vertical or horizontal).  \n   Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies either on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (if\n   \\(a\\le n-1\\)) or on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (if \\(a=n\\)).  Hence\n   \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and gives a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n3. **A sunny line that can replace a non‑sunny one.**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) consider the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i^{(1)}=(i,1),\\qquad P_i^{(2)}=(i+1,2).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Both belong to \\(S_n\\) because \\(i+1+2\\le n+1\\) (the inequality is\n   equivalent to \\(i\\le n-2\\); for the remaining value \\(i=n-1\\) we use\n   \\(P_{n-1}^{(2)}=(n,1)\\), which is already on the horizontal line\n   \\(y=1\\)).  \n   The line through \\(P_i^{(1)}\\) and \\(P_i^{(2)}\\) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y=x-i+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_i\\) is **sunny** (it is neither\n   horizontal, vertical nor parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  Moreover \\(\\ell_i\\)\n   contains exactly the two points \\(P_i^{(1)}\\) and \\(P_i^{(2)}\\).\n\n4. **Replacing a vertical line by a sunny one.**  \n   In the family \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) the vertical line \\(x=i\\) covers the set  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le n+1-i\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   After we delete the line \\(x=i\\) we keep all the other vertical lines,\n   the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and, if necessary, we add the sunny line\n   \\(\\ell_i\\) defined in (4).  The points of \\(V_i\\) are now covered as\n   follows:\n\n   * the point \\((i,1)=P_i^{(1)}\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\);\n   * the point \\((i+1,2)=P_i^{(2)}\\) also lies on \\(\\ell_i\\);\n   * every remaining point \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\ge 2\\) and \\((i,j)\\neq (i+1,2)\\)\n     satisfies \\(i+j\\le n+1\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal line\n     \\(x+y=n+1\\).  We keep this diagonal line in the family at all times.\n\n   Consequently the deletion of the vertical line \\(x=i\\) and the insertion\n   of the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) preserve the covering of all points of\n   \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Keeping the number of lines equal to \\(n\\).**  \n   The diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) is **non‑sunny** (its slope is \\(-1\\)), but\n   it is indispensable for the covering after any replacement described in\n   step 4.  Hence we keep it permanently.  Starting from \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) we\n   have \\(n\\) lines; after each replacement we still have exactly \\(n\\) lines\n   (one line is removed, one is added).\n\n6. **Realising any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   The original family \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n   By performing the replacement of step 4 on any subset of the\n   vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) we turn precisely the chosen\n   vertical lines into sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\).  The horizontal line\n   \\(y=1\\) may also be turned sunny: replace it by the line \\(y=x\\), which\n   passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) and is sunny; the points that were\n   covered only by \\(y=1\\) are now covered either by the diagonal\n   \\(x+y=n+1\\) (the point \\((n,1)\\)) or by a vertical line that has