\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\), and this bound is attained by the lines \\(y=x-c\\) (or \\(y=x+c\\)).\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   Using the maximal capacities from (2) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot m+(n-k)\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (3) for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\frac{n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-m\\,}\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-m)} .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd*, then \\(m=t+1\\) and \\(n-m=t\\); (4) yields \\(k\\le n\\).  \n   *If \\(n=2t\\) is even*, then \\(m=t\\) and \\(n-m=t\\); (4) yields \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Since we have only \\(n\\) lines altogether, the bounds are\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n      & (n\\text{ odd}),\\\\[2pt]\n   n-1    & (n\\text{ even}).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Base configuration (no sunny line).*  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_0=\n   \\bigl\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   The \\(n-1\\) vertical lines together with the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\); all lines are non‑sunny, so \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) realises \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines of slope \\(1\\).*  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_c:\\; y = x-c .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Each \\(\\ell_c\\) is sunny and contains exactly \\(m=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the points \\((a,a-c)\\) with \\(a\\) ranging as in (2)).  \n\n   *Replacing vertical lines.*  \n   For any integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) replace the vertical line \\(x=r\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\,r-1}\\).  \n   The column \\(x=r\\) consists of the points \\((r,1),(r,2),\\dots ,(r,n+1-r)\\).  \n   The first two of them, \\((r,1)\\) and \\((r,2)\\), lie on \\(\\ell_{\\,r-1}\\); the remaining points of the column satisfy \\(r+(b)=n+1\\) for \\(b=n+1-r\\) and therefore lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   Hence after the replacement the set \\(S_n\\) is still completely covered.\n\n   Performing the above replacement for any collection of distinct columns yields a configuration with exactly as many sunny lines as replacements have been made, while the other lines (the untouched vertical ones and the diagonal) remain non‑sunny.  \n   Consequently, for every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   we obtain a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *The case \\(k=n\\) (odd \\(n\\) only).*  \n   When \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd we can replace **all** \\(n-1\\) vertical lines by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{0},\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n-2}\\) and, in addition, replace the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\,n-1}\\) (which is \\(y=x-(n-1)\\)).  \n   The line \\(\\ell_{\\,n-1}\\) contains the single point \\((n,1)\\); all other points of \\(S_n\\) are covered exactly as in the previous step, because each column \\(x=r\\) is now covered by \\(\\ell_{\\,r-1}\\) (providing the points \\((r,1),(r,2)\\)) and the diagonal points \\((r,n+1-r)\\) are still present on the line \\(\\ell_{\\,n-r}\\) (which coincides with the former diagonal for the appropriate \\(c\\)).  \n   Thus we obtain a configuration consisting of \\(n\\) sunny lines and no non‑sunny line, proving that \\(k=n\\) is attainable for odd \\(n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The inequality (5) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines when \\(n\\) is odd and more than \\(n-1\\) sunny lines when \\(n\\) is even.  \n   The constructions above realise every integer \\(k\\) in the corresponding ranges.  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k=0,1,\\dots ,n\\text{ if }n\\text{ is odd, and }k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\text{ if }n\\text{ is even.}\\;}\n   \\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
