f \\(S_n\\) (the whole first\n   row, the whole first column or the whole diagonal).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot m+(n-k)\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (4) for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\frac{n(n+1)/2-n^{2}}{\\,m-n\\,}\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-m)} .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(m=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) we have \\(n-m\\ge1\\), and (5) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus a configuration can never contain \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n\n   *Base configuration (no sunny line, \\(k=0\\)).*  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{0}\n   =\\bigl\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   The \\(n-1\\) vertical lines together with the diagonal cover all points of\n   \\(S_n\\); all lines are non‑sunny.\n\n   *Sunny lines of slope \\(1\\).*  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{c}:\\;y=x-c .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is sunny (its slope is \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_{c}\\) are exactly those with\n   \\(a-b=c\\); there are at most \\(m\\) of them, and for every admissible\n   \\(c\\) the line indeed contains at least one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *General configuration for a prescribed \\(k\\).*  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Consider the following set of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{k}\n   =\\bigl\\{\\ell_{0},\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{x=k+1,\\;x=k+2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The first \\(k\\) lines are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n   We show that \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *Case 1.* \\(a-b<k\\).  \n   Then