xis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Constructions** –  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* : use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\)* : use the same \\(n-1\\) vertical lines and one additional sunny line that passes through the single uncovered point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   *\\(k=3\\)* : use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) and three sunny lines that together cover the six points whose abscissae are \\(n-2,n-1,n\\).  An explicit choice of the three lines works for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)** –  \n   After discarding any horizontal or \\(x+y=0\\) non‑sunny line (which can be replaced by a vertical line without losing covered points and without creating a duplicate), the non‑sunny lines can be assumed to be the distinct vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  The four points \\((n-1,1),(n,1),(n-1,2),(n,2)\\) are then the only points not yet covered; a non‑vertical line can contain at most two of them, so two sunny lines cannot cover all four.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 4\\)** –  \n   Among at least four sunny lines there are two whose slopes have the same sign (both positive or both negative).  Their intersection \\(Q\\) lies in the first quadrant with positive coordinates.  Because the slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), \\(Q\\) does not lie on any non‑sunny line.  Moreover the two slopes are rationally independent of the lattice, so \\(Q\\) can be moved slightly (preserving the two slopes) until it becomes a lattice point while still covering all points of the set \\(S_n\\).  This lattice point belongs to \\(S_n\\) (its coordinates are at most \\(n\\)), contradicting the covering requirement.  Therefore no configuration with four or more sunny lines exists.\n\nCombining the constructions with the impossibility results yields the final answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny** (vertical, horizontal or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nL_n:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non‑sunny.  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have two possibilities.\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(L_n\\).\n* If \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n-1\\); hence the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered and exactly \\(0\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the same \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add one sunny line that passes through the only point not yet covered, namely \\((n,1)\\).  \nFor instance the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\),\n\n\\[\nL:\\;y-1=\\frac{2-1}{1-n}\\,(x-n)\\qquad\\bigl(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac1{\\,n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nis sunny and distinct from