el to \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(y=-x+c\\)), it contains at most one point of \\(S_n\\) (all points of \\(S_n\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates).  Replacing such a line by the vertical line \\(x=c\\) does not decrease the set of covered points and does not create a duplicate line, because the abscissae \\(c\\) are all different (otherwise the original two non‑vertical lines would already coincide).  Consequently we may assume that the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are the distinct vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-2\\) are already covered by (2).  The only points of \\(S_n\\) that may remain uncovered are\n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\;P_2=(n,1),\\;P_3=(n-1,2),\\;P_4=(n,2).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(If \\(b\\ge3\\) then \\(a\\le n-2\\) and the point is vertical.)\n\nThus the two sunny lines must together contain the four points in (3).  A non‑vertical line can contain at most two of them, because any two of the points have different abscissae.  Hence the two sunny lines can cover at most four points, and to cover all four they would have to be\n\n\\[\nU:\\;P_1P_3,\\qquad V:\\;P_2P_4 .\n\\]\n\nBoth of these lines have slope \\(-1\\); they are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore are **not** sunny – a contradiction.  Consequently no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non‑existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nSuppose that a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least four sunny lines.  Among them there are two, say \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), whose slopes have the same sign (both positive or both negative); this follows from the pigeon‑hole principle applied to the three possible sign patterns \\(\\{+, -\\}\\).\n\nBecause the slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), the intersection point  \n\n\\[\nQ=A\\cap B\n\\]\n\nlies in the first quadrant with strictly positive coordinates, and \\(Q\