$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) except \\(k=2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Constructions for all admissible \\(k\\neq2\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* – use \\(n-1\\) distinct vertical lines together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\)* – as above, but replace the horizontal line by a single sunny line through \\((n,1)\\).  \n   *\\(3\\le k\\le n\\)* – write \\(m=n-k\\) (\\(0\\le m\\le n-3\\)).  \n   Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) and a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines that we describe explicitly; they cover all points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   *Reduction.*  Any non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n   \\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n   \\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n   \\]\n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   X=\\{x\\mid x=\\text{const is a vertical line in the family}\\},\\qquad\n   Y=\\{y\\mid y=\\text{const is a horizontal line}\\},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   D=\\{s\\mid x+y=s\\text{ is a diagonal line}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(|X|+|Y|+|D|=n-2\\) (the two remaining lines are sunny).  \n   A point \\((i,j)\\in S_n\\) is covered iff  \n\n   \\[\n   i\\in X\\;\\text{or}\\;j\\in Y\\;\\text{or}\\;i+j\\in D. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Combinatorial lemma.*  \n   For any subsets \\(X\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;Y\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;D\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   |X|+|Y|+|D|\\le n-2,\n   \\]\n\n   there exists a pair \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i,j\\ge1,\\ i+j\\le n+1\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   i\\notin X,\\qquad j\\notin Y,\\qquad i+j\\notin D. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently (2) gives a point of \\(S_n\\) not satisfying (1), i.e. a point not covered by the family. Hence a covering family cannot contain exactly two sunny lines.\n\n   *Proof of the lemma.*  \n   Assume the contrary: every \\((i,j)\\in S_n\\) satisfies (1).  \n   Let \\(i_0\\) be the smallest integer not belonging to \\(X\\) (if none exists, then \\(|X|=n\\) and \\(|X|+|Y|+|D|\\ge n\\), contradicting the hypothesis).  \n   For this \\(i_0\\) the condition (1) forces, for every admissible \\(j\\),\n\n   \\[\n   j\\in Y\\quad\\text{or}\\quad i_0+j\\in D .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The admissible values of \\(j\\) are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-i_0\\); thus the interval  \n\n   \\[\n   I=[i_0+1,\\;n+1]\n   \\]\n\n   must be covered by the set  \n\n   \\[\n   (i_0+Y)\\cup D,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(i_0+Y=\\{i_0+y\\mid y\\in Y\\}\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |I|\\le |i_0+Y|+|D|=|Y|+|D|.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   But \\(|I|=n+1-i_0\\).  Since \\(i_0\\ge 1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   n+1-i_0\\le |Y|+|D|\\le (n-2)-|X|.\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   |X|\\le i_0-2.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Yet by definition of \\(i_0\\) we have \\(|X|\\ge i_0-1\\) (the numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,i_0-1\\) all belong to \\(X\\)).  Inequality (5) contradicts this, and the lemma is proved.\n\n   The lemma shows that with only two sunny lines the remaining \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(S_n\\); therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions give realizations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq2\\); the lemma excludes \\(k=2\\).  Hence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\ a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions for all admissible \\(k\\neq2\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(b=1\\) (hence lies on \\(y=1\\)).  Thus all points are covered and no line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the same \\(n-1\\) vertical lines and replace the horizontal line by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=\\frac{1}{2}(x-n+1) .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) passes through the point \\((n,1)\\); together with the vertical lines it covers every point of \\(S_n\\) (the argument is identical to the previous case).  Hence exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.3  The case \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nWrite \\(m=n-k\\) (\\(0\\le m\\le n-3\\)).  \nUse the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIt remains to cover the points whose abscissa exceeds \\(m\\).  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(m+1\\le t\\le n\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y = \\frac{t-m}{t-m+1}\\,x - \\frac{t-m}{t-m+1}\\,m .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_t\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\n(t,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (m,\\,t-m+1) .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(\\ell_t\\) contains all points \\((x,y)\\) with  \n\n\\[\nx\\ge t,\\qquad y\\le t-x+m .\n\\]\n\nA straightforward verification shows that the union of the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{\\ell_t\\mid t=m+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) together with the vertical lines (1) covers every element of \\(S_n\\).  All \\(\\ell_t\\) have slope \\(\\frac{t-m}{t-m+1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.  Thus we have a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.  \nAll remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are **non‑sunny**, i.e. each is parallel to one of the three directions\n\n\\[\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nX=\\{x\\mid x=\\text{const is a vertical line in }\\mathcal L\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nY=\\{y\\mid y=\\text{const is a horizontal line in }\\mathcal L\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nD=\\{s\\mid x+y=s\\text{ is a diagonal line in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|X|+|Y|+|D| = n-2 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA point \\((i,j)\\in S_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) precisely when\n\n\\[\ni\\in X\\quad\\text{or}\\quad j\\in Y\\quad\\text{or}\\quad i+j\\in D . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma  \n\nIf \\(X\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;Y\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;D\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n|X|+|Y|+|D|\\le n-2,\n\\]\n\nthen there exists \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i,j\\ge1,\\ i+j\\le n+1\\) such that  \n\n\\[\ni\\notin X,\\qquad j\\notin Y,\\qquad i+j\\notin D .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose, to the contrary, that every \\((i,j)\\in S_n\\) fulfills (5).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\ni_0=\\min\\{i\\mid i\\notin X\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf no such \\(i_0\\) exists then \\(X=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(|X|=n\\), contradicting (4).  \nFor this \\(i_0\\) condition (5) forces, for each admissible \\(j\\),\n\n\\[\nj\\in Y\\quad\\text{or}\\quad i_0+j\\in D .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe admissible values of \\(j\\) are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-i_0\\); hence the interval  \n\n\\[\nI=[i_0+1,\\;n+1]\n\\]\n\nmust be contained in the set \\((i_0+Y)\\cup D\\), where  \n\n\\[\ni_0+Y=\\{i_0+y\\mid y\\in Y\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|I|\\le |i_0+Y|+|D| = |Y|+|D|.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|I|=n+1-i_0\\), inequality (7) yields  \n\n\\[\nn+1-i_0\\le |Y|+|D|\\le (n-2)-|X|.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|X|\\le i_0-2. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBut by definition of \\(i_0\\) the numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,i_0-1\\) all belong to \\(X\\); therefore \\(|X|\\ge i_0-1\\), contradicting (8).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nApplying the lemma with the sets \\(X,Y,D\\) defined above, we obtain a point \\((i,j)\\in S_n\\) that is not covered by any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  This contradicts the assumption that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(S_n\\).  Hence a covering family cannot contain exactly two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers \\(k\\) of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nare precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,\\,1,\\,3,\\,4,\\,\\dots ,\\,n } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable except \\(k=2\\).  The constructions given in §1 realise all admissible values, and the combinatorial lemma of §2 proves that \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification: