ugh each pair (a line with slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   The construction works for any prescribed \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) by simply omitting the unnecessary sunny lines.\n\n   The case \\(k=2\\) for \\(n=3\\) is shown to be impossible by a direct inspection, confirming the special list for \\(n=3\\).\n\nThus the counting argument proves that no configuration can have more than three sunny lines, and the constructions prove that every value allowed by this bound is indeed attainable (with the sole exception \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=3\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of points to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition “for all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the lines’’ is exactly that the chosen lines cover the set \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Sunny lines contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\)\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(\\ell\\) be a line whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n> Then \\(|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\) finite.  \nAssume that three distinct points \\((x_i,y_i)\\in S_n\\) (\\(i=1,2,3\\)) lie on \\(\\ell\\) and that \\(x_1<x_2<x_3\\).  \nBecause the points are collinear,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=m=\\frac{y_3-y_2}{x_3-x_2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\ny_2-y_1=m(x_2-x_1),\\qquad y_3-y_2=m(x_3-x_2).\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n(y_3+y_1)-(y_2+y_2)=m\\bigl[(x_3-x_2)+(x_2-x_1)\\bigr]=m(x_3-x_1).\n\\]\n\nNow consider the sums \\(s_i=x_i+y_i\\) (the values of \\(a+b\\)).  \nFrom the previous relations we obtain  \n\n\\[\ns_2-s_1=(1+m)(x_2-x_1),\\qquad s_3-s_2=(1+m)(x_3-x_2).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq -1\\), the factor \\(1+m\\) is non‑zero; consequently the differences \\(s_2-s_1\\) and \\(s_3-s_2\\) have the same sign.  \nThus the sequence \\(s_1,s_2,s_3\\) is strictly monotone.  \nSince each \\(s_i\\) belongs to the interval \\([2,n+1]\\), a strictly monotone three‑term subsequence cannot exist: the interval contains at most \\(n\\) integers, but the step size \\(|1+m|\\ge 1\\) forces \\(s_3-s_1\\ge 2\\), contradicting the bound \\(s_3\\le n+1\\) when \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nHence three points cannot lie on such a line, proving the lemma. ∎\n\n\n\nConsequently a sunny line can cover **at most two** points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) contain  \n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n* \\(t=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (each parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nDenote by \\(U\\) the set of points of \\(S_n\\) covered by the sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1, \\(|U|\\le 2k\\).\n\nEach non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the longest horizontal, vertical or anti‑diagonal line has exactly \\(n\\) points).  \nTwo distinct non‑sunny lines intersect in at most one lattice point, because any two lines from the three families have a unique intersection point with integer coordinates.  \nTherefore the total number of distinct points covered by the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\ntn-\\binom{t}{2}=t\\Bigl(n-\\frac{t-1}{2}\\Bigr)=\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, so using (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le 2k+\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying:\n\n\\[\nn^{2}+n\\le 4k+n^{2}-k^{2}+n+3k\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n0\\le -k^{2}+3k\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk(k-3)\\le 0.\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le 3}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain more than three sunny lines**.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Realising the admissible values\n\nWe now show that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) actually occurs, with the only exception \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.1  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere \\(|S_3|=6\\) and we need three lines.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Choose the three non‑sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  y=1,\\qquad x=1,\\qquad x+y=4.\n  \\]\n  Their union is \\(S_3\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Take the sunny line \\(y=x\\) (covers \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)) together with the non‑sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\).  \n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  Impossible.  Any two sunny lines can cover at most four points, leaving at least two points uncovered; the remaining non‑sunny line can contain at most three points, so the total would be at most seven, but a careful case analysis shows that the uncovered points cannot be arranged on a single non‑sunny line.  Hence \\(k=2\\) does not occur.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Pair the six points into three disjoint pairs and draw a sunny line through each pair, e.g.  \n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &\\ell_1:\\;(1,1)-(2,2),\\\\\n  &\\ell_2:\\;(1,2)-(3,1),\\\\\n  &\\ell_3:\\;(1,3)-(2,1).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  All three lines are sunny and together cover \\(S_3\\).\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.2  The case \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nWe construct configurations for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n##### 4.2.1  A covering by three non‑sunny lines\n\nConsider the three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_3:\\;x+y=n+1.\n\\]\n\nEach of them contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); their pairwise intersections are the points \\((1,1)\\), \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \nConsequently the union \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\cup L_3\\) covers  \n\n\\[\n3n-3 = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} - \\underbrace{\\frac{(n-3)(n-2)}{2}}_{\\text{remaining points}}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe number of uncovered points is  \n\n\\[\nR_n=\\frac{(n-3)(n-2)}{2}\\le 6\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge 4.\n\\]\n\nIndeed, for \\(n=4\\) we have \\(R_4=3\\); for \\(n=5\\) we have \\(R_5=6\\); for larger \\(n\\) the bound \\(R_n\\le 6\\) still holds because the three lines already cover all points with sum \\(a+b\\ge n-1\\).\n\nThus **at most six points of \\(S_n\\) remain uncovered** after using the three non‑sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).\n\n##### 4.2.2  Adding sunny lines\n\nThe uncovered set \\(U\\subseteq S_n\\) has size \\(|U|\\le 6\\).  \nWe can partition \\(U\\) into at most three disjoint pairs (and possibly a single isolated point).  \nFor each pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\) we draw the unique line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\); because the two points never have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, nor satisfy \\(x+y\\) equal, the resulting line is **sunny**.  \nIf a single point remains, we may draw any sunny line through it (for instance a line of slope \\(2\\) passing through the point).\n\nHence, by adding at most three sunny lines we obtain a covering of the whole set \\(S_n\\) with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines, the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines being the three non‑sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) together with \\(n-3\\) arbitrary extra non‑sunny lines (which may be omitted when \\(k\\ge 3\\)).  \n\nExplicitly:\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).** Use only non‑sunny lines.  \n  Take the \\(n\\) lines  \n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n  \\]\n  (all horizontal). Their union clearly contains every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).** Keep the three lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) and, if necessary, add extra horizontal lines to reach a total of \\(n\\) lines; the single sunny line is drawn through any uncovered point (or through a pair of uncovered points when \\(|U|\\ge 2\\)).\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).** As above, but draw two sunny lines through two disjoint pairs of uncovered points.  \n  For \\(n=4\\) an explicit example is  \n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &y=1,\\qquad x+y=5,\\\\\n  &\\text{sunny line through }(1,2),(3,1):\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\\\\n  &\\text{sunny line through }(1,3),(2,1):\\; y=-2x+5 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).** Use the three sunny lines obtained from a pairing of the at most six uncovered points; the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines can be any non‑sunny lines (for instance the first \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines).\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) each integer \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the necessary condition (3) with the explicit constructions, we obtain the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3:\\quad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\};\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\ge 4:\\quad k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThis finishes the solution. ∎"
