h the single point (any line of slope, say, \\(2\\) passing through it).  \n   * By adding \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) of those sunny lines we obtain a covering of the whole set \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the remaining \\(n-3-(k-0)\\) lines may be taken arbitrarily among the horizontal family).  \n\n   Thus every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is realizable for all \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\nThe revised construction removes the false claim that three particular non‑sunny lines leave at most six uncovered points for every \\(n\\); instead we use **all** horizontal lines up to \\(y=n-3\\), which guarantees the uncovered set has size exactly six for any \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem requires a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines whose union contains every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Sunny lines contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\)\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(\\ell\\) be a line whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) (i.e. a sunny line). Then \\(|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\) finite.  \nAssume three distinct points \\((x_i,y_i)\\in S_n\\) (\\(i=1,2,3\\)) lie on \\(\\ell\\) and that \\(x_1<x_2<x_3\\).  \nCollinearity gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=m=\\frac{y_3-y_2}{x_3-x_2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\ny_2-y_1=m(x_2-x_1),\\qquad y_3-y_2=m(x_3-x_2).\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities yields  \n\n\\[\ny_3-y_1=m(x_3-x_1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(s_i=x_i+y_i\\) (the value of \\(a+b\\) for the point).  \nFrom the first equality we obtain  \n\n\\[\ns_2-s_1=(1+m)(x_2-x_1),\\qquad s_3-s_2=(1+m)(x_3-x_2). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq-1\\), the factor \\(1+m\\) is non‑zero; consequently the signs of the two differences in (3) are the same, so the sequence \\(s_1,s_2,s_3\\) is strictly monotone.  \n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le s_i\\le n+1\\).  \nA strictly monotone three‑term integer sequence contained in an interval of length \\(n-0\\) must have a step of at least \\(1\\); thus \\(s_3-s_1\\ge2\\).  \nBut from (2) we have  \n\n\\[\ns_3-s_1=(x_3+y_3)-(x_1+y_1)= (x_3-x_1)+(y_3-y_1)= (x_3-x_1)+m(x_3-x_1)=(1+m)(x_3-x_1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|1+m|\\ge1\\) and \\(x_3-x_1\\ge2\\), we obtain \\(s_3-s_1\\ge2\\), which forces \\(s_3\\ge n+2\\) when \\(n\\ge3\\), contradicting \\(s_3\\le n+1\\).  \nHence three points cannot be collinear on a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\nThus a sunny line can cover **at most two** points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a configuration contain  \n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n* \\(t=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nThe sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (Lemma 1).\n\nEach non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the longest horizontal, vertical or anti‑diagonal line has exactly \\(n\\) points).  \nTwo distinct non‑sunny lines intersect in at most one lattice point, because any two lines taken from the three families intersect uniquely.  \nTherefore the total number of distinct points covered by the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\ntn-\\binom{t}{2}=t\\Bigl(n-\\frac{t-1}{2}\\Bigr)=\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, so using (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n0\\le -k^{2}+3k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k(k-3)\\le0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le3}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNo configuration can contain more than three sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Realising the admissible values  \n\n#### 4.1  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(|S_3|=6\\). A direct inspection yields:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three non‑sunny lines \\(y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=4\\).\n* \\(k=1\\): sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\).\n* \\(k=2\\): impossible (any two sunny lines cover at most four points, leaving at least two points that cannot lie on a single non‑sunny line).\n* \\(k=3\\): three sunny lines through the three disjoint pairs  \n  \\((1,1)-(2,2),\\;(1,2)-(3,1),\\;(1,3)-(2,1)\\).\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible set is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The case \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n##### 4.2.1  A universal non‑sunny covering\n\nTake the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-3).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_i\\) is non‑sunny.  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-3\\) lie on one of these lines.  \nThe points that are **not** covered are precisely those with \\(b\\in\\{n-2,n-1,n\\}\\).  \nBecause of the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), they are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n& (1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\;(3,n-2),\\\\\n& (1,n-1),\\;(2,n-2),\\\\\n& (1,n-2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus at most six points remain uncovered, **independently of the size of \\(n\\)**.\n\n##### 4.2.2  Adding sunny lines\n\nThe set (6) contains at most six points, so we can partition it into at most three disjoint pairs; the line through each pair has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (the two points never share the same \\(x\\), the same \\(y\\), nor satisfy \\(x+y\\) equal), hence each such line is sunny.  \n\nIf the number of points in (6) is odd, one point is left alone; we draw any sunny line through that single point (for instance the line of slope \\(2\\) passing through it). This adds exactly one sunny line.\n\nConsequently, by adding\n\n* **0** sunny lines we obtain a covering with \\(k=0\\);\n* **1** sunny line (drawn through any pair of points of (6) if \\(|(6)|\\ge2\\), otherwise through the single point) we obtain \\(k=1\\);\n* **2** sunny lines (through two disjoint pairs) we obtain \\(k=2\\);\n* **3** sunny lines (through three disjoint pairs) we obtain \\(k=3\\).\n\nThe total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-3) \\;+\\; k \\;=\\; n,\n\\]\n\nso the requirement of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines is satisfied.  \nAll lines are distinct because the sunny lines are not horizontal, while the \\(n-3\\) non‑sunny lines are all different horizontal lines.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge4\\) each integer \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is realizable.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe counting argument forces \\(0\\le k\\le3\\).  \nThe explicit constructions above show that\n\n* for \\(n=3\\) the attainable values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\);\n* for every \\(n\\ge4\\) all four values \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) occur.\n\nHence the complete description of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n\\ge4.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]  \n\n∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
